RocksDB stores value in terms of byte array so normal rocksdb fetch would give the value in byte array --> converted to json (standard java) --> c
onverted to POJO (jackson ) --> application logic to desired form --> User 

Object cache (like guava) will store the POJO (basically Jackson output) directly for a key so that the calls below it are not made 
If object cache hit the flow is : Request --> ObjectCache we get the POJO --> application logic to desired form --> USER

Kryo operates at a level lower than this , it stores the deserialzied output of byte array so we essentially skip the part of Jackson and it can convert this 
deserialzied form to POJO 

If kryo cache hit the flow is : Request --> Kryo cache (we get serialized response) and we convert it to POJO --> app logic --> USER
So it essentially replaces byte Array <--> POJO

*** Correction ***

Kryo doesn’t store "deserialized output" but rather stores serialized binary data that can be quickly deserialized into the original POJO. This eliminates the need for Jackson and JSON conversions altogether.

Kryo is a high-performance serialization framework that operates at a binary serialization level, as opposed to JSON (text-based serialization).
If you use Kryo as a cache serializer, the serialized form stored in the cache is not JSON, but a Kryo-serialized binary format. This allows faster deserialization compared to JSON-based approaches.

Without Kryo: RocksDB (byte array) → JSON → POJO (Jackson)
With Kryo: RocksDB (byte array) ↔ POJO (Kryo)
